{
  "name": "The Riverlight Masquerade (one-evening taster)",
  "tone": "politic",
  "startingLocation": "The Lantern Terrace",
  "startingRegion": "The Silted Reaches, a confederation of river-lords who control trade along the Marrowfen River",
  "premise": "River-Lord Ossvane hosts a masquerade to bind five river-holds against ruinous upstream tariffs. The alliance pools barges and bargains as one; tonight's assent begins cooperation, not instant control of upstream tolls. Lady Threnna Voss has invited the party as independent observers and offers 10 gold for an honest report by the toast or its agreed postponement, payable whether or not they solve the case. On the Lantern Terrace at 21:00 she shows them an anonymous warning: 'Petrell's coat will condemn them at the toast.' She wants the claim checked before anyone is publicly accused. Establish the job, coat and deadline immediately; the party may decline or leave.\n\nGM TRUTH: Devrin Sallowmere commissioned scribe Mira Fen, attending in a moth mask as a visiting collector, to counterfeit a treaty annex. It appears to give Petrell secret exemptions from the shared tariffs. Devrin planted it in Petrell's blue coat in the Glass Conservatory at 20:40. The genuine treaty remains in Ossvane's locked dais chest; there is no swap and no missing original seal. Devrin plans to reveal the annex at midnight, have Petrell excluded, and offer his own barges as the alliance's indispensable carrier. Petrell owes him money but never agreed to the annex.\n\nIndependent treaty witness Orris Vale saw Devrin plant the annex, checked its false dock clause, and challenged him in the library. Devrin left, requested a candle at 20:45, then returned and stabbed Orris at 20:50; Orris is already dead at the opening. Mira saw the planting, sent Threnna the warning, and fears being next. She did not witness the murder. Fix these facts before play; never move guilt to match a player's accusation. The five featured masked guests are Threnna, Devrin, Petrell, Mira and Orris. Ossvane is the host, Brix the steward; background delegates are not extra secret culprits.\n\nThe treaty needs all five holds' assent. One credible discrepancy can pause ratification while it is checked; corroborated evidence can settle matters early. Guarding the genuine treaty alone cannot stop a public frame-up. The shortest complete route is the opening, the coat and counting-house, then a settlement; investigating Orris adds an answer to the murder. End with the actual consequences of the party's choices, not a compulsory sequel.",
  "acts": [
    {
      "title": "Act One: The Masks Arrive",
      "goal": "Give the party a paid invitation, a specific warning and a visible midnight deadline. Introduce the four living featured guests by mask and motive; the fifth, Orris Vale, is missing from the receiving line. The party can pursue the coat immediately. A body discovery supplies another lead, not permission to begin investigating.",
      "turningPoint": "At 21:15 an available house attendant finds Orris in the East Wing Library unless the party found the body sooner. Brix takes charge if present; otherwise Ossvane assigns another attendant. The host secures the room and asks for witnesses. Report the discovery through someone who knows it. The investigation and any earned early settlement continue; do not resurrect an absent actor or teleport the party to the body.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "An Invitation and a Warning",
          "type": "social",
          "objective": "Open on the Lantern Terrace at 21:00 with Threnna, her warning about Petrell's coat, and her offer of 10 gold for an honest report by the toast or its agreed postponement. She explains that failed agreement keeps food and freight expensive. Tell the player the coat is in the Glass Conservatory; Brix can verify documents. Introduce other guests only as they approach: Threnna wears a reed mask, Devrin a silver heron, Petrell a blue fish, Mira a moth. Orris's white owl mask is absent. Offer the coat, Brix, or introductions as immediate choices. Declining the fee does not end play: they may help voluntarily, mingle or leave. Offer an epilogue only when they choose to leave or end the evening.",
          "dnaHint": "Resolve this opening when the invitation and warning are understood and the party chooses its next step, including voluntary help or declining the job. Acceptance is not required. Introduce the next beat through something witnessed or reported, not a mandatory tour of all guests."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Empty Owl Mask",
          "type": "investigation",
          "objective": "In the East Wing Library, Orris lies dead with one stab wound. An inspection reveals a torn silver-heron sleeve button clutched in their hand and a note written in their own blood: \"Sallowmere planted it. Blue coat. He came back.\" Their name is on the witness register; Brix identifies the handwriting. Neither clue vanishes on a failed roll. Searching Orris's papers reveals that the altered annex refers to a dock absent from the signed treaty. Devrin's missing button and fresh scratch corroborate contact, not by themselves murder. The party may leave the murder unresolved while protecting the alliance.",
          "dnaHint": "This lead resolves when the party investigates the body or entrusts it to the household and chooses another concern. An available attendant can report the discovery. Closing the lead is not solving the killing; leave unexamined clues unknown and the inquest unresolved. Never require a library visit to proceed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Act Two: Behind the Masks",
      "goal": "Follow any useful leads in any order. The coat proves a forgery exists; the payment and signed order establish its commissioner; Mira can independently corroborate the planting. Two corroborating routes are enough for exposure, and a single credible discrepancy earns time to check it. No arc is a compulsory visit. If evidence or negotiation resolves the dispute early, move directly to the settlement.",
      "turningPoint": "When the party establishes the annex is planted, make the actual threat clear: Devrin intends to accuse Petrell of cheating the alliance, not exchange the genuine treaty. This can emerge from his order, Mira's testimony, Orris's note, or comparing the annex with Petrell's account. The contact is never the sole source of the revelation.",
      "parallel": true,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Cards, Coats and Alibis",
          "type": "social",
          "objective": "The card table is in the Grand Hall, not another required room. Threnna can show her months-old receipt and plainly marked reference copy; neither contains the new dock exemption. Petrell admits owing Devrin money if offered discretion, denies agreeing to an annex, and permits inspection of their blue coat. Devrin claims he stayed at cards after 20:30, but Brix's service record places him requesting a library candle at 20:45. Observers can notice his missing heron button and fresh wrist scratch. Do not make mind-reading, intimidation, or a particular question necessary to find ordinary contradictions.",
          "dnaHint": "This optional lead resolves when investigated, explicitly declined, genuinely unavailable, or made unnecessary by evidence the party accepts as sufficient. State which happened. Close resolved leads before advancing the act; never invent a visit, testimony or clue to justify closure. A player still pursuing a live question may continue it."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Steward's Ledgers",
          "type": "investigation",
          "objective": "Brix grants access on Threnna's introduction or an explained warning. His ledger records Devrin paying Mira this morning for an \"annex correction\". Mira lodged the signed commission in a sealed packet as security for the unpaid balance; Brix logged it unopened. Evidence of forgery or danger to Mira warrants opening it. Inside, Devrin directs her to invent Petrell's exemption and copy the treaty seal. Brix authenticates his handwriting against ordinary signed receipts; the receipt alone proves no crime. Copies and testimony preserve the evidence. The debt ledger separately explains Petrell's fear. If Brix is absent, Ossvane can authorize inspection and compare the specimens. Mira need not be present.",
          "dnaHint": "This optional lead resolves when investigated, explicitly declined, genuinely unavailable, or made unnecessary by evidence the party accepts as sufficient. State which happened. Close resolved leads before advancing the act; never invent a visit, testimony or clue to justify closure. A player still pursuing a live question may continue it."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Coat in the Conservatory",
          "type": "exploration",
          "objective": "Petrell's blue coat hangs on a numbered peg in the Glass Conservatory; the false annex is inside its inner pocket from 20:40 until somebody moves it. An attentive search finds it without a roll. Permission from Petrell, Threnna or Ossvane opens the room; asking the lantern-boy, sneaking or forcing entry are alternatives with stated social risks. Brix or Ossvane can compare the annex with the genuine treaty: wrong dock name, false Petrell signature, copied seal with an extra reed. Finding it proves forgery, not Petrell's or Devrin's guilt. Track who takes it. Devrin cannot produce a second copy if the party secures this one.",
          "dnaHint": "This optional lead resolves when investigated, explicitly declined, genuinely unavailable, or made unnecessary by evidence the party accepts as sufficient. State which happened. Close resolved leads before advancing the act; never invent a visit, testimony or clue to justify closure. A player still pursuing a live question may continue it."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Moth at the Water-Gate",
          "type": "social",
          "objective": "Mira waits in the Water-Gate Gardens for the 22:30 supply boat. She admits being the scribe if offered safe company with Brix or protection from Devrin; no payment, capture or combat is required. She saw Devrin plant the annex and can identify his signed commission and her own work. She wrote Threnna's warning. She did not see Orris die and will not invent eyewitness testimony. If she departs, refuses, or dies, the coat, signed order and Orris evidence remain. If the party helps her escape, distinguish protecting a witness from concealing her role. Devrin does not acquire her knowledge automatically.",
          "dnaHint": "This optional lead resolves when investigated, explicitly declined, genuinely unavailable, or made unnecessary by evidence the party accepts as sufficient. State which happened. Close resolved leads before advancing the act; never invent a visit, testimony or clue to justify closure. A player still pursuing a live question may continue it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Act Three: The Midnight Toast",
      "goal": "Resolve the frame-up and the treaty through the evidence and choices actually made. The party may expose Devrin publicly, negotiate safeguards, ask for a delay, make an unsupported accusation, or leave. A confession, arrest, every clue, and solving the murder are not prerequisites to protecting Petrell. The final gathering can ratify an earlier settlement rather than reveal a new culprit.",
      "turningPoint": "At midnight Devrin tries to produce the annex if he possesses it or can actually retrieve it. Its last established custodian and any access restrictions govern; he cannot conjure another copy. If it is secured away from him or destroyed, he may allege misconduct but must face the evidence already heard. Honor any earlier settlement or legitimate postponement. Resolve an unresolved case using the actual witnesses, documents and choices, not a compulsory failure.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "The Case Before the Toast",
          "type": "social",
          "objective": "Let the party act when ready. The annex plus Brix's authenticated commission proves Devrin ordered the fraud without Mira or a confession. Mira's planting testimony corroborated by Orris's note or the commission also supports exposure. Keep murder separate: Orris's dying note, torn button and Devrin's contradicted alibi support his detention for the killing; a button alone does not. One credible clue lets Ossvane pause ratification for a stated check. Without proof, guests ask for verification rather than becoming certain. Devrin may deny, bargain or flee; evidence still counts. No automatic duel, omniscient countermeasure or new mastermind rescues his scheme.",
          "dnaHint": "Resolve this arc when the party chooses a settlement, accepts a postponement, or deliberately declines intervention. A costly or partial outcome counts as a resolved decision. No confession, arrest or successful treaty is required. Then narrate the consequences in the closing arc."
        },
        {
          "title": "What the River Carries Home",
          "type": "social",
          "objective": "Give a short epilogue matching the outcome rules: public exposure; a safeguarded private settlement; unsupported or corrected accusation; or no intervention. State whether all five holds assented, whether Petrell was protected or excluded, what happened to Devrin and Mira, and whether Orris's killing was proved, referred for investigation or left unresolved. Do not reveal unearned secret knowledge through the player. Threnna pays the agreed 10 gold if the party reports honestly, even after failure. Close on lanterns reflected in the river and one concrete effect on the people trading there. Ask whether the player wants to end the taster; further play is optional.",
          "dnaHint": "Resolve this arc after the agreed outcome and immediate consequences have been shown, including an honest unresolved inquiry. Complete the authored evening and let the player decide whether to end or continue. Do not seed a compulsory new case or treat further play as necessary to finish this one."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "deepTime": [
    {
      "name": "The Marrowfen Dominion",
      "when": "over six centuries ago",
      "note": "a single river-spanning kingdom that dammed and tamed the Marrowfen before collapsing into warring holds"
    },
    {
      "name": "The Century of Nine Weirs",
      "when": "roughly two hundred years ago",
      "note": "the era when nine river-lords fought over dam rights until only five holds survived, each claiming a stretch of the Marrowfen"
    },
    {
      "name": "The Tariff Years",
      "when": "the last decade",
      "note": "living memory of upstream tolls imposed by outside merchant-crowns, which the treaty being signed tonight was drafted to end"
    }
  ],
  "npcs": [
    {
      "name": "River-Lord Ossvane",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "Host in a plain gold half-mask. Wants all five holds to ratify the alliance and fears another season of costly freight. He keeps the genuine treaty in the locked Grand Hall dais chest; the seal is intact. He has the key and allows a witnessed comparison when shown a discrepancy. One credible clue earns a specific check and a delay; two corroborating sources can expose the fraud without a confession. He does not knowingly condemn an innocent guest to save appearances. If Devrin escapes, he can still record the evidence and exclude him as carrier. Ordinary house guards protect people; they are not a mandatory boss fight.",
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "Lady Threnna Voss",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "Reed mask; a small hold's delegate who remembers freight costs as empty supper plates. She invited the party as independent observers and offers 10 gold for an honest report by the toast or its agreed postponement, payable even if they fail. Mira's anonymous warning reached her at 20:55: \"Petrell's coat will condemn them at the toast.\" Threnna does not know its author at first. Her innocent reference copy was commissioned months ago and is plainly labelled COPY; its receipt and ordinary dock terms clear her. She assists a reasonable inquiry without a password, backs safe passage for a witness, and can introduce the party to Brix. She cannot pardon murder or forgive Petrell's private debt.",
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "Master Devrin Sallowmere",
      "role": "villain",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "Silver heron mask; merchant-financier, charming when he controls the room. Commissioned Mira's false annex, planted it in Petrell's blue coat at 20:40, and stabbed Orris in the library at 20:50 after Orris saw him plant it. His sleeve lost a heron button in the struggle; his wrist is scratched. He falsely claims continuous attendance at cards after 20:30. Mira secretly lodged his signed commission with Brix as payment security; Devrin does not know this or which clues the party has found. Excluding Petrell would make his barges the alliance's main carrier. He may deny, offer to abandon that bid, or flee a supported accusation; he has no backup annex, hostage army or second mastermind. He cannot erase secured evidence or force a duel.",
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "Guest-Lord Petrell Sae",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "they/them",
      "notes": "Blue fish mask; a minor hold-lord who keeps smoothing their empty cuffs. Their blue coat hangs in the Glass Conservatory. They owe Devrin money but never signed a tariff exemption or hired Mira. They will permit the party to inspect the coat when told of the warning and cooperate with a discreet investigation. Being indebted is not proof of forgery. Clearing their name does not cancel the debt; Threnna can arrange an honest repayment negotiation after the toast without making it a required sequel. They want both their hold's vote and a way to look their neighbors in the eye.",
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "Mira Fen",
      "role": "wildcard",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "Moth mask; real name Mira Fen, a hired scribe posing as a visiting collector. She knowingly forged Devrin's annex and saw him put it in Petrell's coat. Fearing for her safety, she sent Threnna the anonymous warning, then went to the Water-Gate Gardens to catch the 22:30 supply boat. She did not witness the murder and owns no stolen original seal. Protection beside Brix or voluntary safe company is enough for cooperation; coin and violence are unnecessary. She left Devrin's signed commission sealed with Brix as payment security and can authenticate it and describe the planting. She accepts being held as a witness if protected, or escapes if ignored. Her testimony supports the other clues; losing her never erases them.",
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "Steward Halven Brix",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "Unmasked steward with an ink-stained thumb. Keeps payment records, a service log and Mira's sealed commission packet in the Counting-House. She lodged it as security for Devrin's unpaid balance; he logged it unopened. Evidence of forgery or danger to Mira justifies opening it. He knows Devrin's handwriting from ordinary signed receipts and can compare specimens. Threnna's introduction or an explained warning gains access. He recognizes Orris's handwriting from the witness register. He responds to the 21:15 body discovery if available; his log places Devrin requesting a library candle at 20:45, contradicting the card-table alibi. If absent, records and Ossvane's inspection remain available.",
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "Orris Vale",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "they/them",
      "notes": "White owl mask; independent treaty witness and the fifth featured guest. Already dead at the 21:00 opening, killed by Devrin in the East Wing Library at 20:50; never introduce Orris alive. After seeing the planting at 20:40, they checked the annex and challenged Devrin in the library. He left, then returned at 20:50. During the stabbing they tore off his silver-heron sleeve button. Before dying they wrote in blood: \"Sallowmere planted it. Blue coat. He came back.\" Brix recognizes their hand from the witness register. Their papers note the false annex's nonexistent dock. If an established ability permits communication with the dead, honor its limits: Orris knows the planting and their attacker, not the payment terms or Mira's later movements.",
      "armor": "none"
    }
  ],
  "locations": [
    {
      "name": "The Lantern Terrace",
      "description": "Paper lanterns hang over black river water smelling of reeds and woodsmoke. This is the 21:00 opening: Threnna meets her invited observers here with the warning and offers their job. Glass doors lead into the Grand Hall; an outdoor path reaches the Water-Gate Gardens. A servant with Orris's entry in the guest register can explain which owl-masked guest is missing. Distances around this single manor are minutes, not expeditions."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Grand Hall",
      "description": "A river-stone ballroom with a musicians' gallery, food from all five holds and a card table along the west wall. Ossvane's locked dais chest holds the intact genuine treaty; he has its key. A curtained arch opens to the Glass Conservatory, an east passage to the library, and courtyard doors face the Counting-House. The final dance is announced at 23:00, guests are called at 23:45, and the toast is at midnight unless a specific check delays it."
    },
    {
      "name": "The East Wing Library",
      "description": "River charts and old ledgers surround a cold hearth. Orris lies dead beside the reading desk, white owl mask fallen away, torn heron button in hand, blood-written note beside them. Their witness papers are on the desk. A cracked window overlooks the servants' path, but it is not proof of an escaping intruder. The door is unlocked tonight. The party can inspect the body and papers respectfully without winning a roll to notice the core clues."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Counting-House",
      "description": "Across the Grand Hall courtyard, Brix keeps trade ledgers, Mira's sealed commission packet, Devrin's ordinary signed receipts and the service log. The packet was lodged as payment security; evidence of forgery or danger to Mira warrants opening it. Threnna's older copy receipt is filed separately. Brix grants access when the warning is explained; if absent, Ossvane can authorize inspection. Copies preserve evidence if an original is later lost. Breaking in remains an alternative with announced risks."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Glass Conservatory",
      "description": "Warm citrus trees and damp glass soften the music from the Grand Hall. Petrell's blue coat hangs on a numbered peg, the planted annex inside until somebody moves it. A lantern-boy tends the lamps; he is an ordinary servant, not a combat encounter. He allows access with Petrell's, Threnna's or Ossvane's permission. The window shows the Water-Gate Gardens below. Opening the pocket, examining the false annex and retaining it are separate choices; track actual custody."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Water-Gate Gardens",
      "description": "Terraces of reeds and hedge walls descend from the Lantern Terrace to a private dock. Mira waits here for the 22:30 supply boat; its lantern and bell announce its arrival. It is a normal boat, not a hidden assassin's escape trap. A path returns to the terrace and hall. Calm conversation can secure Mira's voluntary help. If the boat leaves with her, the other evidence remains where the party left it."
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    "Fixed mystery: Devrin commissioned and planted the false annex and killed Orris before the opening. The genuine treaty is never swapped. Do not change the culprit or revive Orris for plot convenience, add a backup forgery or invent a new mastermind. Honor established abilities and their actual limits; any earned return to life changes events rather than retconning the murder.",
    "Opening: 21:00 on the Lantern Terrace. The party are invited observers, not prisoners. Threnna offers 10 gold for an honest report by the toast or its agreed postponement, even after failure. Her warning names Petrell's coat. Declining the fee leaves voluntary help or attendance open; offer an epilogue when the player chooses to leave or end the evening.",
    "Clock: boat at 22:30, final dance at 23:00, summons at 23:45, toast at 00:00. Use elapsed fictional time, never a hidden message count. Announce approaching events. Before a lengthy action, state its time cost and the deadline it risks. Dialogue and ordinary inspections do not consume surprise hours.",
    "Pacing: the coat and counting-house support a short route. Resolve optional leads as investigated, declined, unavailable or superseded, stating which; never invent discoveries for unvisited leads. Close resolved arcs before advancing the act. An early solution moves to settlement, not a compulsory tour.",
    "Clues: deliberate inspection reveals core documents and visible physical evidence without a roll. Rolls can change discretion, time or extra detail after stakes are stated; a failed roll never erases the sole clue. Preserve discovered facts and the actual custody of documents and witnesses.",
    "Proof: a false annex proves forgery, not its owner's guilt. The annex plus Brix's authenticated fraudulent order exposes Devrin; Mira's planting account plus Orris's note or the order is another route. A credible single clue warrants a check or pause. Confession and arrest are never necessary to establish the fraud.",
    "Murder is a separate finding: Orris's note, the button torn from Devrin's sleeve and his contradicted alibi corroborate his guilt. A scratch or button alone is insufficient. If proof is incomplete, say the killing is unresolved and give the evidence to Brix; do not invent a confession or require a sequel.",
    "At the toast, track actual custody: Devrin can use the annex if he holds it or can retrieve it, including if the party gave it to him. Secured or destroyed evidence cannot reappear. Copies and witnessed discoveries still count. Ossvane may postpone ratification for a specific check; honor an earlier settlement.",
    "Public exposure: corroborated fraud clears Petrell; all five holds may ratify with their assent. Devrin loses the carrier bid even if he escapes. Murder evidence governs detention or inquiry; Mira is not automatically pardoned. Shared shipping can begin at dawn, while lower upstream tolls still require negotiation.",
    "Private settlement: secure the annex, obtain Devrin's withdrawal from the carrier bid and protect Petrell's vote before claiming success. If he refuses, pause ratification or go public. A bargain cannot erase Orris's death; report the actual evidence to Brix. Concealing a known killer is the party's choice, with that moral cost stated.",
    "Wrong accusation: distinguish asking questions from declaring guilt. Unsupported claims prompt verification, not automatic punishment or a false confession. Threnna's older labelled copy can clear her. Allow a correction before ratification. If trust collapses and a hold withholds assent, the treaty is delayed and tariffs continue.",
    "No intervention: with no settlement, the accusation or unresolved killing may cause the holds to postpone ratification; tariffs continue. Petrell faces suspicion, not automatic conviction. Devrin remains answerable to any evidence, Mira leaves if able, and the killing may remain under inquiry. Offer an ending without forcing further play.",
    "Endings follow actual choices: if the annex is secured but its author unknown, Ossvane rejects the unproved charge; willing holds may ratify under document checks, while the commission and murder remain unresolved. Consent matters: no outcome forces a delegate's signature. Close the evening with a brief honest epilogue.",
    "People are not clue dispensers or mandatory fights. Reasonable requests, cooperation, safe passage and established character abilities can work. Do not charge a bribe for an essential clue or require violence against Mira. If a witness dies, leaves or refuses, records and other evidence remain; never fabricate their testimony.",
    "Nobody has plot armor. If a delegate dies or cannot assent, the treaty is delayed unless that hold can legitimately authorize another signer; do not invent instant consent. If the host or steward is lost, surviving witnesses and secured records remain, but missing authority or proof must have honest consequences.",
    "NPC initiative: a discovered killing prompts the host to secure the library and hear witnesses even if the party does nothing. Brix or another attendant reports only what they actually find; they may preserve evidence and recommend postponement. Never make competent witnesses ignore a clue just to reserve its discovery for the player."
  ],
